WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.548
The year is 1986.

00:00:02.014 --> 00:00:03.902
You work in Tony Pawson's laboratory in Toronto.

00:00:04.395 --> 00:00:08.552
A graduate student is changing a stretch of
about 100 amino acids, a few at a time.

00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:14.309
This stretch sits right beside the kinase domain
of a tyrosine kinase from a cancer virus.

00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:20.480
The same stretch is found beside the kinase in
every cytoplasmic tyrosine kinase sequenced so
far.

00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:22.394
No one knows what it does.

00:00:22.898 --> 00:00:25.263
The student has two results, and they disagree.

00:00:26.057 --> 00:00:30.991
1. In a test tube, the changed protein adds
phosphate to tyrosine at the normal rate.

00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:36.835
2. In cells, the virus with the changed protein
does not transform the cells into cancer cells.

00:00:37.398 --> 00:00:42.625
So the method that the field trusts for finding
the job of part of a protein shows nothing
wrong.

00:00:43.095 --> 00:00:44.187
Answer two questions.

00:00:44.695 --> 00:00:50.782
1. How can these 100 amino acids matter inside a
cell, when they change nothing in the test tube
assay?

00:00:51.595 --> 00:00:53.768
2. What must the region recognize?

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.723
What would you purify and test against it to
find out?

00:00:57.074 --> 00:01:01.853
The 100 amino acids fold into a shallow pocket,
and the pocket binds a phosphate.

00:01:02.535 --> 00:01:05.367
This region is now called the SH2 domain.

00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:10.408
In 1992, crystallographers studied the structure
of one of these domains.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.465
They found a group of arginine and lysine side
chains, which carry positive charges.

00:01:15.973 --> 00:01:22.537
These side chains closed around a single
phosphate group on a single tyrosine of another
protein, and held it.

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:26.427
The domain holds on until a phosphatase removes
that phosphate.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.456
Then the two proteins come apart.

00:01:28.937 --> 00:01:30.743
That is the whole job of the domain.

00:01:31.048 --> 00:01:38.017
It brings its kinase next to a particular
protein that has just been phosphorylated, and
it holds the two together.

00:01:38.891 --> 00:01:43.966
If the domain is broken, the kinase works as
before, but nothing is brought within its reach.

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:49.644
Phosphate on a tyrosine works well as a binding
site because it is cheap and reversible.

00:01:50.255 --> 00:01:54.079
Kinases add it, and phosphatases remove it,
within seconds.

00:01:54.584 --> 00:01:59.338
So a cell can switch a binding site on and off
without making or destroying any protein.
